  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1147 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nNear record breaking temperatures remain the story across the Mid-\nSouth as a 590dam ridge remains parked over the Lower Mississippi\nValley. Taking a look at 00Z surface analysis, a stationary\nboundary is extending across the Ohio River Valley through the\nSouthern Plains, just north of Springfield, MO. This front will\nbring our first shot at isolated to scattered precipitation\nchances in about 10 days Monday afternoon into evening. Forecast\nPWATs also begin to increase to around 1.75\", nearing the 75th\npercentile for this time of the year, supporting increased\nmoisture. However, latest HREF probabilities only have around a\n20% chance of exceeding a quarter of an inch of rainfall Monday.\nThe environment in the afternoon into early evening hours will be\nsupportive of wet microbursts with ample instability in place,\nbut little to no shear. A strong thunderstorm or two with strong\nwind gusts can't be ruled out, but nothing to write home about.\nAs far as temperatures go Monday, we're looking at yet another\nnear record-breakingly hot September day with highs in the mid \n90s to lower 100s.\n\nOn the bright side, relief from 48 days of 90 degree or higher\ntemperatures is close by as a cooler airmass filters in behind\nthe aforementioned front. We're looking at highs in the 80s to\nlower 90s by Tuesday as the zonal flow also begins to push in\naloft. A few lingering showers and thunderstorms may be witnessed\nnear the Tennessee River and over northeast Mississippi through\nTuesday afternoon, although nothing appreciable is expected once\nagain. Wednesday looks even more pleasant with lows in the 60s\nand highs in the 80s across much of the Mid-South as a cutoff low\ndips across the northern Ohio River Valley and upper-level\nnorthwest flow begins to move in over the Middle Mississippi\nValley. Ensemble members are in decent agreement with upper-level\nnorthwest flow remaining over the region through at least the\nweekend, keeping temperatures near normal. As far as\nprecipitation goes past Tuesday, we're looking at a relatively\ndry period through at least the weekend as dry air filters in and\nsurface high pressure moves in behind the aforementioned front.\n\nAEH",
